{
  "image_name": "0d6e2507dc600b786115192a288715e3.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure outlines a pipeline for developing immunotherapies from a patient’s tumour tissue. Tumour samples are analyzed at several molecular levels—DNA, RNA, and what appears to be an HLA ligandome—to discover tumour-specific targets. These candidate targets are then filtered through a target identification step that combines automated and manual curation.\n\nOnce targets are selected, the lower half shows several therapeutic formats that can be built from them. One route is personalized vaccines, depicted as different product types labeled SDP, UADP, and UDP, which seem to be administered back to the patient. Another route is engineered cell therapy: patient immune cells are collected by leukapheresis, modified by transfection to express a receptor, and reinfused as CAR T cells. The figure also includes TCR-mimic antibodies that bind peptide–HLA complexes on tumour cells, and bispecific TCR molecules that link a tumour target to CD3 on T cells to drive immune activation.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes an integrated precision-oncology workflow: starting from multi-omics tumour profiling, identifying actionable antigens, and translating them into multiple immune-based treatment strategies.",
